  "prose": "At North Quay Station, Mara held the archive telegram beneath the platform clock and read the line again. “Arrival recorded: 02:10.” The wording belonged to the archive, she reminded herself; it did not by itself establish that anything had physically arrived.\n\nTeodor Vane stood beside the bench, his pocket watch open in his palm. “I waited on the platform,” he said. “I saw no train at 02:10.”\n\nMara glanced at the watch. Its hands remained at 02:07. “But your watch stopped at 02:07.”\n\n“Yes.” Vane closed the case with his thumb. “That is what it shows.”\n\n“Then it can bound what you timed,” Mara said, “not prove what the train did afterward.”\n\nShe turned to the platform clock’s strike record and traced the entry for train 41. There was no mark. The telegram listed an arrival; the mechanical record carried no corresponding strike. Neither record, she wrote, could be promoted into the other kind of evidence merely by standing beside it.\n\nMara opened her notebook and wrote: “Archive telegram: arrival recorded at 02:10. Platform clock: no mark for train 41. Vane: reports waiting and seeing no train; pocket watch stopped at 02:07.” She paused, then added, “Discrepancy documented; explanation unknown.”\n\nVane watched the pencil move. “So what do you report?”\n\n“What the sources say, and what they cannot establish,” Mara answered. “The archive lists an arrival. The platform record does not mark one. Your stopped watch limits your timing. That is the gap.”"
